{
  "requirement": "Describes one step from a selector’s root. `base` formats the kind and label. The optional one-based ordinal disambiguates siblings with the same base.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct Segment {\n    pub kind: String,\n    pub label: Label,\n    pub ordinal: Option<usize>,\n}\n\nimpl Segment {\n    pub fn new(kind: String, label: Label) -> Self;\n    pub fn ordinal(self, ordinal: usize) -> Self;\n    pub fn base(kind: &str, label: &Label) -> String;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for Segment;\nimpl Debug for Segment;\nimpl Display for Segment;\nimpl PartialEq for Segment;\nimpl Eq for Segment;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject is `requirements.sdk.outlines.Segment`. The reviewed tests are `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/selector.rs#mod[tests]/fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]`, which is marked {test} for the subject, and `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`, which checks the public export inventory. I read the implementation only to see which paths the test reaches. I judged expected behavior from the requirement text.\n\nObligations and the tests that cover them:\n- **Fields `kind`, `label` and `ordinal: Option<usize>`.** The test asserts `segments[0].kind == \"impl\"`, `segments[0].label == Label::Named([...])`, `segments[1].ordinal == Some(2)` and `plain.ordinal == None`. It also asserts `segments[2].label == Label::Hash(\"0123456789ab\")`.\n- **`Segment::new(kind, label)`.** The test builds `plain` with `new`, asserts that its ordinal is `None`, and asserts that `base` of its kind and label gives `fn[run]`.\n- **`ordinal(self, n)`.** The test asserts that `plain.ordinal(2).to_string() == \"fn[run]#2\"`. It also asserts that `segments[2].clone().ordinal(3)` displays as `use@0123456789ab#3`, which shows that an ordinal replaces and appends to an existing segment.\n- **`base(kind, label)` formats kind and label.**\n  - Named label: the test asserts `base(\"impl\", [\"a/b\",\"c d\"]) == \"impl[a%2Fb/c%20d]\"`. This checks the bracketed form, the part join and the encoding.\n  - Hash label: `Display` is asserted for the hash segment, `use@0123456789ab`. `Display` calls `base`, so this covers the `kind@hash` form.\n  - Ordinal exclusion: `base` is asserted without an ordinal.\n- **`Display`.** The test asserts the output with an ordinal (`fn[run]#2`, `use@...#3`) and without one (`use@0123456789ab`).\n- **`Clone` and `PartialEq`/`Eq`.** The test calls `clone()`. It also asserts that `numbered == segments[1]`, so a constructed segment equals a parsed one.\n- **`Debug`.** No test checks it. It is a plain `#[derive]` with no conditional or transformation, so it needs no test of its own.\n- **`#[non_exhaustive]` and the public signatures.** `public_exports_match_the_appendix` checks the SDK export inventory and that the shipping consumer compiles against the SDK.\n\nI found no requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept.",
  "references": "f65a79860c11538c0d1ddd31f000335aacb8b48d92b940aa758fb0218c520eba",
